 - The training data is small (a few hundred rows at most). Use low-capacity, regularised models: ridge or additive models on level or descriptor features, with a limited number of pairwise interactions that you chose. Small ensembles (random forest or gradient boosting, shallow) are acceptable for choosing batches, but the final model has to be expressible in plain JavaScript. A table of coefficients and a few arithmetic operations is the safest choice.
 - Select models by cross-validation that matches the real test. Use grouped folds that hold out whole groups where that mimics the held-out set (for example, leave-one-substrate-out, leave-one-element-out). Score with NDCG@k using the task's relevance definition, not R². Spearman correlation is a useful secondary check.
+- **Forward validation.** Random-fold CV on data you chose adaptively is optimistic. From round 2 onward, also fit on everything before the latest round and score how that model ranks the latest round's results (task metric where computable, plus Spearman and top-k precision). The latest batch is the closest thing to unseen pool data you have. Keep it as a second opinion next to grouped CV, label every figure in `notes.md` as forward, grouped, random-fold or in-sample, and do not compare figures of different kinds.
+- **Capacity check.** Whenever you consider a hierarchical, interaction-heavy or otherwise higher-capacity variant, evaluate it against a plain additive model with the same validation. Ship the higher-capacity one only if it wins by more than the noise you can see (for example, across folds or bootstrap refits). A model that looks better only on its own training data or on a proxy score you built is not evidence.
+- **Resolution diagnostic.** Equal predictions keep evaluation order, so a model that maps many different designs to the same value wastes ranking resolution. Before delivering, count distinct predictions over the full catalogue, find the largest tied block and see where it sits in the ranking. A big block near or above the middle of the ranking, or any tie inside the part of the list you believe is the top, is a reason to add a small smooth term (a per-amount or per-level term, a shrunk similarity to observed points). Do not add random noise. This is a diagnostic, not a rule: judge it by what it does to the forward score, not by a threshold.
 - If two models score within noise of each other, choose the simpler one. With few observations of a noisy target, differences of a few hundredths in NDCG are noise.
 - Measurement noise matters. Where replicate standard deviations are given, don't trust small differences between candidates. Prefer features that explain broad trends.
 - Treat each prior (physics-based descriptor, a trend from the screen on a different substrate) as a feature with a regularised weight. Don't hard-code it as a rule. Let the data in the pool shrink it.
